{% capture filters %}
[
  {%- for item in data.filters -%}
    {% liquid
      assign first_param_type = item.params[0].types[0] | downcase | default: 'untyped'
      # `object` becomes `variable`, which is what `hash` used to become here, and it is not
      # cosmetic: the language server offers a filter for the value piped into it by testing
      # `syntax.startsWith(inputType)`, and `variable` is its name for the "accepts anything"
      # bucket. `PseudoType` has no `object` member, so `options('object')` is never called —
      # published verbatim, `object | hash_merge` matches no bucket at all and the 25 filters
      # taking a hash or an untyped value would quietly stop being offered anywhere.
      #
      # The platform's unified vocabulary publishes `object` where this document used to receive
      # `hash` (16 filters) and `untyped` (10), so this one branch covers both old spellings and
      # keeps the published `syntax` identical to what production serves today. Teaching the
      # language server to infer `object` instead is the better end state — TASK-51.1's other half.
      case first_param_type
      when 'object'
        assign first_param_type = 'variable'
      endcase

      assign default_description = 'returns ' | append: item.returns[0].description
    %}
    {
      {%- comment -%}
        `data.filters` is now the WHOLE vocabulary — the platform's filters and Liquid's own, both
        documented in the application repository (`platform_filters.rb` and
        `docs/liquid/standardfilters.rb`) and serialized into one array by the YARD generator. There
        is no second list to concatenate and no name that can appear in both, so this partial
        publishes every filter a template can call.

        `platformOS` distinguishes them, and is present only on the platform's own: the language
        server reads the KEY's presence to pick the reference page to link, so publishing `false`
        would send every core filter to the platformos-filters page.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "category": {% if item.platformOS %}"platformOS"{% else %}"liquid"{% endif %},
      {%- comment -%}
        `deprecated` is a boolean and the reason is the text, both straight from the
        generator. This used to read `item.deprecated.size > 0` and publish
        `item.deprecated | join: ', '` as the reason, which was the contract from before the
        generator emitted a boolean — and a boolean has no `size`, so EVERY filter published
        `deprecated: false`, while the reason became the stringified boolean. 116 filters
        published the reason `"false"` and the six genuinely deprecated ones published `"true"`,
        so the only way downstream could tell a deprecated filter from a current one was to
        match that word. The prose the generator had all along was dropped on the floor.

        This is the same defect the tags partial carries a note about having fixed. It was
        fixed there and not here.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "deprecated": {{ item.deprecated | default: false | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        Read through under the name it is published as. The generator used to emit
        `deprecated_reason`/`deprecated_replacement` and this partial renamed them, so the only
        statement that the two spellings are one field lived here, in a third repository — and
        anything consuming the platform's filters.json directly lost both fields with nothing to
        notice. The generator now emits `deprecation_*` itself.

        The `deprecated_*` fallback is for a filters.json produced by a platform version from
        before that change, which is the same reason the reason field has a fallback at all.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "deprecation_reason": {{ item.deprecation_reason | default: item.deprecated_reason | default: '' | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        The successor's registered NAME, for a consumer that acts on the deprecation instead of
        displaying it: platformos-check offers to rename a deprecated filter to it. That rename
        was matched out of the English reason with a "replaced by [`name`]" pattern which none
        of the six real reasons uses — so it was never once offered, and nothing failed, because
        a fix that is not suggested looks exactly like a fix nobody asked for.

        Blank for a filters.json published by an older platform version, which is the same
        fallback path the reason still serves.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "deprecation_replacement": {{ item.deprecation_replacement | default: item.deprecated_replacement | default: '' | json }},
      "description": {{ item.description | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        Was `item.decription` — a typo, so the value was always blank and the fallback always
        won. The fallback reads the RETURN description, which is the only sentence most of these
        filters have: 92 of 122 carry no `@description` at all.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "summary": {{ item.description | default: default_description | json }},
      "syntax": "{% print first_param_type %} | {% print item.name %}",
      {% if item.platformOS %}"platformOS": true,{% endif %}
      "name": {{ item.name | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        `{% if %}`, not `| default: '[]'`. An empty array is BLANK in Liquid, so `default:`
        substitutes the string `'[]'`, and `| json` then publishes the two-character string "[]"
        where an array belongs. Every filter without aliases got that, and a consumer iterating
        the field saw two entries, `[` and `]`.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "aliases": {% if item.aliases %}{{ item.aliases | json }}{% else %}[]{% endif %},
      {%- comment -%}
        How many arguments the filter accepts, the piped input included. `FilterArity` in
        platformos-check refuses a call outside this range and the MCP write gate blocks on it,
        so it is part of the contract, not a display detail.

        It was previously absent, and recovering it downstream meant over-applying all 206
        filters against a live instance and reading the runtime's own complaint — which could
        not answer for the four that take a splat. `max` is null for exactly those: it means
        "cannot refuse an argument", which is a different statement from "unknown".

        `{% if %}` rather than `default:` because 0 is a legitimate `max` and is truthy in
        Liquid, while nil is not — `default:` would turn a real 0 into null.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "arity": {
        "min": {{ item.arity.min | json }},
        "max": {% if item.arity.max %}{{ item.arity.max | json }}{% else %}null{% endif %}
      },
      "parameters": [
        {% for param in item.params %}
          {
            "description": {{ param.description | json }},
            "name": {{ param.name | json }},
            {%- comment -%}
              Was hardcoded `false` for every parameter of every filter, which is the reason
              arity had to be measured downstream instead of read. It comes from the method
              signature now, not from the annotation: 13 filters have an `@param` list that
              disagrees with the parameters they actually take, `encode` documenting its two
              encoding arguments in the opposite order to the one it accepts.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            "required": {{ param.required | default: false | json }},
            "variadic": {{ param.variadic | default: false | json }},
            "default": {{ param.default | default: '' | json }},
            {%- comment -%}
              "pass this by position, not as `name: value`". The language server reads
              `!positional` as "this is a named argument", so while the field was absent every
              filter argument was offered as `name: value`, including the ones that only work
              positionally.

              Defaults to true rather than false: Liquid cannot reach a Ruby keyword parameter at
              all, so a published filter parameter is always positional, and for a filters.json
              from before the generator emitted this, true is the answer for every filter in it.
              The named arguments a filter really accepts are its trailing options Hash, whose
              keys are the `@option` entries — not these.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            "positional": {{ param.positional | default: true | json }},
            "types": {% if param.types %}{{ param.types | json }}{% else %}[]{% endif %}
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ],
      "return_type": [
        {% for return_type in item.returns %}
          {
            "type": "{% print return_type.types | join: ', ' | downcase %}",
            "name": "",
            "description": {{ return_type.description | json }},
            "array_value": ""
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ],
      "examples": [
        {% for example in item.examples %}
          {
            "name": "",
            "description": "",
            "syntax": "",
            "path": "",
            "raw_liquid": {{ example | json }},
            "parameter": false,
            "display_type": "text",
            "show_data_tab": true
          }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {% endfor %}
      ]
    }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
  {% endfor %}
]
{% endcapture %}
{% liquid
  assign filters = filters | parse_json: raw_text: true

  return filters
%}
